WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.156 --> 00:00:03.117
- Hi everyone, Sal Khan
here from Khan Academy.

00:00:03.117 --> 00:00:06.007
Welcome to Ed talks on Khan Academy.

00:00:06.007 --> 00:00:06.917
I know what you're thinking.

00:00:06.917 --> 00:00:08.977
What are these Ed talks?

00:00:08.977 --> 00:00:11.007
Well, this is kind of a subset

00:00:11.007 --> 00:00:14.287
of the Homeroom with Sal conversations

00:00:14.287 --> 00:00:16.487
that are more focused on education

00:00:16.487 --> 00:00:18.447
or from experts or specialists

00:00:18.447 --> 00:00:19.857
in the field of education.

00:00:19.857 --> 00:00:22.427
We have a really exciting conversation,

00:00:22.427 --> 00:00:23.767
but before we do that,

00:00:23.767 --> 00:00:26.087
I'm gonna give you my
standard announcements.

00:00:26.087 --> 00:00:28.297
First of all, a reminder
that we are not for profit.

00:00:28.297 --> 00:00:30.857
We can only exist through
philanthropic donations

00:00:30.857 --> 00:00:31.937
from folks like yourself.

00:00:31.937 --> 00:00:33.627
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:33.627 --> 00:00:38.627
please think about going
to Khan academy.org/donate

00:00:38.627 --> 00:00:42.574
and any donation amount makes
a huge, huge difference.

00:00:42.574 --> 00:00:46.377
Also I wanna give a special
shout out to many corporations

00:00:46.377 --> 00:00:48.937
who've really stepped up
and helped Khan Academy out

00:00:48.937 --> 00:00:50.867
during the COVID crisis.

00:00:50.867 --> 00:00:53.197
We were running a deficit
even before the crisis.

00:00:53.197 --> 00:00:56.177
That deficit only grew as
our traffic to our site

00:00:56.177 --> 00:00:58.747
went to about 250% of normal,

00:00:58.747 --> 00:01:00.377
special thanks to Bank of America,

00:01:00.377 --> 00:01:03.830
google.org, AT&amp;T, Fastly and Novartis.

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:05.637
Also wanna announce that there's a,

00:01:05.637 --> 00:01:08.837
you can get a version of this
and the Homeroom with Sal

00:01:09.837 --> 00:01:12.527
live streams in "Homeroom
with Sal," the podcast,

00:01:12.527 --> 00:01:15.387
wherever you might find your podcasts.

00:01:15.387 --> 00:01:18.325
So with that, I am excited to introduce

00:01:18.325 --> 00:01:22.107
Elisa Villanueva Beard,
CEO of Teach For America.

00:01:22.107 --> 00:01:24.177
Elisa, thanks for coming.

00:01:24.177 --> 00:01:25.987
- Hey Sam, thanks for having me.

00:01:25.987 --> 00:01:27.337
It's good to be here

00:01:27.337 --> 00:01:29.807
and congratulations on all the work

00:01:29.807 --> 00:01:31.047
that you all have been leading.

00:01:31.047 --> 00:01:33.157
It's been inspiring to see

00:01:33.157 --> 00:01:34.797
how you all have been meeting the moment

00:01:34.797 --> 00:01:37.330
and so happy to be here
and in this discussion.

00:01:38.317 --> 00:01:41.060
- And likewise and maybe a
good place to start with.

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:42.337
There's a ton of questions I wanna ask

00:01:42.337 --> 00:01:44.227
and I wanna encourage everyone watching.

00:01:44.227 --> 00:01:46.517
They can go on the message
boards on Facebook or YouTube,

00:01:46.517 --> 00:01:47.857
wherever they're watching this.

00:01:47.857 --> 00:01:49.867
And they can ask questions that hopefully

00:01:49.867 --> 00:01:52.104
we'll be able to surface to Elisa,

00:01:52.104 --> 00:01:54.967
but also just to get
everyone on the same page,

00:01:54.967 --> 00:01:56.447
explain to everyone watching

00:01:56.447 --> 00:01:59.857
what the focus of Teach For America is.

00:01:59.857 --> 00:02:01.177
I think most people have heard of it.

00:02:01.177 --> 00:02:03.247
It's one of the nonprofits
that I've always looked up to

00:02:03.247 --> 00:02:04.767
when I started Khan Academy.

00:02:04.767 --> 00:02:08.040
But tell me in your own words,
what is Teach For America?

00:02:09.427 --> 00:02:11.847
- Well, our focus is working

00:02:11.847 --> 00:02:13.757
alongside many partners to take

00:02:13.757 --> 00:02:16.407
on educational inequity
at the highest level.

00:02:16.407 --> 00:02:19.347
So what we know for sure
at Teach For America

00:02:19.347 --> 00:02:21.957
is that potential is distributed equally

00:02:21.957 --> 00:02:23.917
across all lines of difference,

00:02:23.917 --> 00:02:26.067
but the opportunity and the access

00:02:26.067 --> 00:02:28.667
to meet that potential does not

00:02:28.667 --> 00:02:31.457
exist with all kids in all communities

00:02:31.457 --> 00:02:32.767
in our country.

00:02:32.767 --> 00:02:34.547
And long before COVID,

00:02:34.547 --> 00:02:38.187
our system was not serving
many kids well enough.

00:02:38.187 --> 00:02:40.567
And so I think that's
really important for folks

00:02:40.567 --> 00:02:41.400
to understand.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:42.991
And just to ground folks in that,

00:02:42.991 --> 00:02:46.477
when you look at our
lowest income communities

00:02:46.477 --> 00:02:49.107
who are disproportionally
children of color,

00:02:49.107 --> 00:02:51.774
50% of kids are graduating
from high school

00:02:51.774 --> 00:02:53.987
and that's compared to 96% of kids

00:02:53.987 --> 00:02:55.692
in the most affluent communities

00:02:55.692 --> 00:02:57.857
and have those kids
graduating from high school,

00:02:57.857 --> 00:03:01.267
about 6% we get to and through college.

00:03:01.267 --> 00:03:02.167
And Teach For America

00:03:02.167 --> 00:03:04.727
exists to say that is not okay

00:03:04.727 --> 00:03:07.617
and are committed to
creating a different reality.

00:03:07.617 --> 00:03:10.727
And so we know that
the problem is systemic

00:03:10.727 --> 00:03:12.087
and there needs to be change

00:03:12.087 --> 00:03:13.747
within and outside of education

00:03:13.747 --> 00:03:15.977
to really lead to a different reality.

00:03:15.977 --> 00:03:17.367
We know many interventions

00:03:17.367 --> 00:03:21.124
are needed in order to
be able to take this on.

00:03:21.124 --> 00:03:23.457
And our contribution is leadership.

00:03:23.457 --> 00:03:27.477
So we go out and we recruit
support and develop,

00:03:27.477 --> 00:03:30.947
exceptional equity oriented leaders

00:03:30.947 --> 00:03:32.757
who teach for at least two years

00:03:32.757 --> 00:03:35.007
in urban and rural America.

00:03:35.007 --> 00:03:36.797
They're expected to,

00:03:36.797 --> 00:03:39.667
ensure that our kids get
the education they deserve.

00:03:39.667 --> 00:03:43.297
And through that are able to
get this clarity of conviction

00:03:43.297 --> 00:03:45.731
and understanding of the system,

00:03:45.731 --> 00:03:49.127
the possibility of the
greatness of our children

00:03:49.127 --> 00:03:52.887
and become lifelong advocates
and TJ jets in education.

00:03:52.887 --> 00:03:54.277
We've been around for 30 years.

00:03:54.277 --> 00:03:57.577
We're now 65,000 strong
core members and alumni

00:03:57.577 --> 00:03:59.957
and our folks even 10 of our folks

00:03:59.957 --> 00:04:02.617
in our alumni network
work within education,

00:04:02.617 --> 00:04:05.007
or have a career serving
a low income community,

00:04:05.007 --> 00:04:06.567
which is incredible because most folks

00:04:06.567 --> 00:04:09.417
that joined us had no intention
of taking on this career.

00:04:11.667 --> 00:04:13.577
- Just to give people a perspective,

00:04:13.577 --> 00:04:15.557
when you go to an education circles,

00:04:15.557 --> 00:04:17.127
people who are leading change

00:04:17.127 --> 00:04:20.357
either directly, or even
now they work in industry,

00:04:20.357 --> 00:04:23.017
but on the philanthropic
side or the policy side,

00:04:23.017 --> 00:04:25.957
you meet, Teach For
America alumni everywhere.

00:04:25.957 --> 00:04:28.607
So y'all have been definitely the alumni,

00:04:28.607 --> 00:04:30.357
the leadership is happening.

00:04:30.357 --> 00:04:31.607
And how does that happen?

00:04:32.457 --> 00:04:34.197
You're recruiting students coming

00:04:34.197 --> 00:04:36.997
primarily out of college
and graduate schools

00:04:36.997 --> 00:04:39.357
with, for any young people listening,

00:04:39.357 --> 00:04:41.883
either high school students
or college students,

00:04:41.883 --> 00:04:46.080
how do they become part of
this Teach For America core?

00:04:47.337 --> 00:04:50.597
- Yeah, our mandate is to go out and say,

00:04:50.597 --> 00:04:54.547
get young people, 75% of our
core are right out of college.

00:04:54.547 --> 00:04:56.647
And so we're just trying
to get people to see

00:04:56.647 --> 00:04:58.807
this is amongst the greatest
injustices of our time.

00:04:58.807 --> 00:05:01.727
There's no way for a country to thrive,

00:05:01.727 --> 00:05:04.967
to be able to ensure that
everyone can fully contribute

00:05:04.967 --> 00:05:07.787
without ensuring every child has access

00:05:07.787 --> 00:05:10.607
to an excellent and equitable education.

00:05:10.607 --> 00:05:12.637
And to take that on and
this young generation

00:05:12.637 --> 00:05:16.507
really understands the nature
of the systemic problem.

00:05:16.507 --> 00:05:18.887
We find that folks have on their mind,

00:05:18.887 --> 00:05:22.127
the need to take on climate
change, income, inequality,

00:05:22.127 --> 00:05:24.933
unemployment, systemic racism.

00:05:24.933 --> 00:05:27.877
And there's many ways to take that on.

00:05:27.877 --> 00:05:29.827
And one thing that we try to say is

00:05:29.827 --> 00:05:31.637
ground zero for all these inequities

00:05:31.637 --> 00:05:33.557
are honestly in the classroom

00:05:33.557 --> 00:05:35.427
because you get to see how all the systems

00:05:35.427 --> 00:05:37.927
in a community collide and end up,

00:05:37.927 --> 00:05:39.257
'cause our kids don't live in vacuums

00:05:39.257 --> 00:05:40.554
and all of that ends up in a classroom

00:05:40.554 --> 00:05:43.617
and you start to see how you have to,

00:05:43.617 --> 00:05:45.867
of course, hold our kids
to the highest expectations

00:05:45.867 --> 00:05:47.797
and also provide all the supports,

00:05:47.797 --> 00:05:50.267
all the unmet needs that
kids come with to school.

00:05:50.267 --> 00:05:52.487
And then you're able to see
like things can be different.

00:05:52.487 --> 00:05:54.257
Our kids can do anything

00:05:54.257 --> 00:05:56.717
and we just have to be able to figure out

00:05:56.717 --> 00:05:58.597
how do these systems interact together?

00:05:58.597 --> 00:05:59.697
What can we do about them

00:05:59.697 --> 00:06:03.327
and work in coalitions
and powerful coalitions

00:06:03.327 --> 00:06:05.287
in common things done for kids.

00:06:05.287 --> 00:06:08.257
And so we're out on college
campuses looking for exceptional

00:06:08.257 --> 00:06:11.617
folks who demonstrate leadership and

00:06:11.617 --> 00:06:16.487
are up for one of the greatest
leadership, challenges ever.

00:06:16.487 --> 00:06:18.527
I will say, even as CEO,

00:06:18.527 --> 00:06:20.857
my hardest job was right out of college,

00:06:20.857 --> 00:06:23.727
which was being a TFA core member in 1998

00:06:23.727 --> 00:06:25.147
in Phoenix, Arizona.

00:06:25.147 --> 00:06:27.927
'Cause it's like the best
preparation to be a CEO,

00:06:27.927 --> 00:06:28.760
I will say.

00:06:29.874 --> 00:06:32.650
- I can only imagine.

00:06:33.887 --> 00:06:36.407
Just fast forwarding to today,

00:06:36.407 --> 00:06:39.227
especially with COVID I am curious,

00:06:39.227 --> 00:06:41.947
we started talking before
we started going live,

00:06:41.947 --> 00:06:45.057
but how has COVID
impacted Teach For America

00:06:45.057 --> 00:06:46.497
in all directions?

00:06:46.497 --> 00:06:48.957
What's going on with your
existing teacher core

00:06:48.957 --> 00:06:50.287
who were in classroom,

00:06:50.287 --> 00:06:53.027
but now I'm guessing doing
primarily distance learning,

00:06:53.027 --> 00:06:55.067
how is that affecting your pipeline

00:06:55.067 --> 00:06:57.030
of finding future leaders?

00:06:58.193 --> 00:07:00.847
- Yeah, so we're in the moment,

00:07:00.847 --> 00:07:04.547
as we all know the greatest
disruption in education

00:07:04.547 --> 00:07:07.967
and in US history, recent
history, certainly.

00:07:07.967 --> 00:07:10.817
And for us, we right away.

00:07:10.817 --> 00:07:15.807
So I talked about, we have
this network of, 65,000 folks.

00:07:15.807 --> 00:07:17.907
We right away knew that,

00:07:17.907 --> 00:07:20.527
we centered on how we were
gonna meet the moment.

00:07:20.527 --> 00:07:23.347
And so we're able to get our community,

00:07:23.347 --> 00:07:26.187
our network, learning together.

00:07:26.187 --> 00:07:28.007
We created an educator resource hub

00:07:28.007 --> 00:07:29.877
to share best practices right away.

00:07:29.877 --> 00:07:32.617
We had a platform where our core members

00:07:32.617 --> 00:07:35.178
and alumni were able to,

00:07:35.178 --> 00:07:37.537
put forth projects that
they were trying to fund

00:07:37.537 --> 00:07:39.467
to just meet the basic needs for kids

00:07:39.467 --> 00:07:42.467
or things that were most
pressing in our communities.

00:07:42.467 --> 00:07:44.637
And so we're able to do that,

00:07:44.637 --> 00:07:46.001
but for us very practically,

00:07:46.001 --> 00:07:48.537
the first big pivot we had to make

00:07:48.537 --> 00:07:50.877
was our summer training.

00:07:50.877 --> 00:07:53.167
And so in March, when we realized that

00:07:53.167 --> 00:07:54.567
we weren't going to be able

00:07:54.567 --> 00:07:55.917
to run summer school programs,

00:07:55.917 --> 00:07:59.817
which is a main component of
how we train our teachers,

00:07:59.817 --> 00:08:02.227
we had to figure out if we were even gonna

00:08:02.227 --> 00:08:04.007
be able to bring in a core,

00:08:04.007 --> 00:08:05.167
because we were gonna have to train them

00:08:05.167 --> 00:08:08.027
in a fundamental,
fundamentally different way.

00:08:08.027 --> 00:08:09.910
And so when we went out
and asked our principals

00:08:09.910 --> 00:08:13.347
and superintendents, do
you want our teachers,

00:08:13.347 --> 00:08:15.408
even though they haven't, taught kids,

00:08:15.408 --> 00:08:17.837
which is core to our model,

00:08:17.837 --> 00:08:19.107
we got a resounding yes.

00:08:19.107 --> 00:08:21.237
Now more than ever, we need your teachers.

00:08:21.237 --> 00:08:25.757
And so we pulled together
just our incredible staff,

00:08:25.757 --> 00:08:29.537
from national teams,
regional teams to design,

00:08:29.537 --> 00:08:31.427
we have our Miami-Dade

00:08:31.427 --> 00:08:34.877
executive director run
our program to design

00:08:34.877 --> 00:08:38.267
a new training curriculum in nine weeks.

00:08:38.267 --> 00:08:42.427
We executed on that and
four weeks and two days

00:08:42.427 --> 00:08:44.867
and it was an incredible endeavor.

00:08:44.867 --> 00:08:47.813
What we saw was our teachers,

00:08:47.813 --> 00:08:51.587
coming together in one
network and feeling,

00:08:51.587 --> 00:08:53.577
really the most prepared we've seen

00:08:53.577 --> 00:08:57.097
in terms of what needs
we were able to meet.

00:08:57.097 --> 00:08:58.087
We spent less money.

00:08:58.087 --> 00:09:00.973
And so we were able to
pivot there and we saw,

00:09:00.973 --> 00:09:05.087
our core members just
rising to the occasion,

00:09:05.087 --> 00:09:08.427
the highest retention we've
had also throughout the summer.

00:09:08.427 --> 00:09:11.557
So folks just so committed as we

00:09:11.557 --> 00:09:12.827
process the world around us,

00:09:12.827 --> 00:09:15.467
knowing, gosh, there's no greater impact

00:09:15.467 --> 00:09:18.887
that one can be making them in
the classroom with students.

00:09:18.887 --> 00:09:21.694
And so it's been quite
inspiring to sort of see

00:09:21.694 --> 00:09:25.370
the staff and the network come
together to meet the moment.

00:09:26.347 --> 00:09:28.137
- Yeah and there's something
powerful about that.

00:09:28.137 --> 00:09:30.527
You like the entire education world

00:09:30.527 --> 00:09:33.097
had to quickly go to virtual instruction,

00:09:33.097 --> 00:09:35.367
which was probably
really good preparation.

00:09:35.367 --> 00:09:36.437
There's a lot of negatives about it,

00:09:36.437 --> 00:09:37.997
but really good preparation
for the teachers

00:09:37.997 --> 00:09:42.287
having to then enter into a
virtual environment right now.

00:09:42.287 --> 00:09:44.257
I'm curious about you
on the training side,

00:09:44.257 --> 00:09:46.937
what do you all focus on in that training?

00:09:46.937 --> 00:09:49.227
And are there things that
y'all do in that training

00:09:49.227 --> 00:09:50.467
that you wish were part of,

00:09:50.467 --> 00:09:53.037
kind of the mainstream schools of ed?

00:09:53.037 --> 00:09:54.807
What would you do if you
could wave a magic wand

00:09:54.807 --> 00:09:56.957
on what happens in all teacher training

00:09:56.957 --> 00:09:58.664
in the US or in the world?

00:09:59.757 --> 00:10:01.877
- Well, there's a few things
Sal that we've learned

00:10:01.877 --> 00:10:04.037
over time that just really matter.

00:10:04.037 --> 00:10:07.327
And so one of the first
things that's so relevant

00:10:07.327 --> 00:10:10.537
to this moment is the
power of relationships.

00:10:10.537 --> 00:10:12.457
And I don't know how that rings to you

00:10:12.457 --> 00:10:15.937
as you hear that, but it's been really

00:10:15.937 --> 00:10:20.457
that is so critical in this very moment.

00:10:20.457 --> 00:10:24.597
Our stats are showing or the
stats that folks are sharing

00:10:24.597 --> 00:10:26.537
is that we have about 3 million kids

00:10:26.537 --> 00:10:30.917
that haven't engaged in
learning over, since March,

00:10:30.917 --> 00:10:32.607
either they don't have devices,

00:10:32.607 --> 00:10:35.987
so about 17 million do not
have connectivity or devices,

00:10:35.987 --> 00:10:39.672
which is, something we just
deeply need to take on.

00:10:39.672 --> 00:10:44.672
But we know that the only way
to engage kids and families

00:10:46.627 --> 00:10:48.887
into learning is relationships.

00:10:48.887 --> 00:10:51.257
And so that's a big
component of just like,

00:10:51.257 --> 00:10:52.107
what does it mean

00:10:53.087 --> 00:10:55.007
to be able to build the trust.

00:10:55.007 --> 00:10:57.787
And I believe change only
happens as fast as there is trust

00:10:57.787 --> 00:10:59.397
and deep relationships.

00:10:59.397 --> 00:11:01.587
And so that is a component of what we do.

00:11:01.587 --> 00:11:05.577
We of course also focus
on, rigorous content.

00:11:05.577 --> 00:11:08.967
And we were able to ensure
that all of our core members

00:11:08.967 --> 00:11:13.037
got some training on
the science of reading.

00:11:13.037 --> 00:11:14.977
And we know that this is just so critical.

00:11:14.977 --> 00:11:17.177
I think every teacher at
school needs to ensure

00:11:17.177 --> 00:11:19.627
that all teachers get access to this.

00:11:19.627 --> 00:11:23.067
We do, training around
computational thinking,

00:11:23.067 --> 00:11:26.707
as we think about 21st
century, preparedness.

00:11:26.707 --> 00:11:29.797
And the fact that 65%
of kids in grade school

00:11:29.797 --> 00:11:31.907
today are gonna seek out jobs

00:11:31.907 --> 00:11:35.887
that we don't even know what
they are today is so critical.

00:11:35.887 --> 00:11:38.997
And so how are we developing
this critical thinking skills

00:11:38.997 --> 00:11:43.347
and the social emotional
skills that are needed.

00:11:43.347 --> 00:11:46.377
We also teach in a trauma informed way.

00:11:46.377 --> 00:11:48.537
And so that's core to the training

00:11:48.537 --> 00:11:52.497
that we are able to provide to
our teachers and the support.

00:11:52.497 --> 00:11:56.477
And especially in this
moment when our kids are

00:11:56.477 --> 00:12:01.334
in the middle of experiencing
severe loss, fear

00:12:01.334 --> 00:12:04.377
and so much uncertainty,

00:12:04.377 --> 00:12:06.247
we've gotta ensure that we're really able

00:12:06.247 --> 00:12:09.797
to meet the basic needs
for kids, the human needs

00:12:09.797 --> 00:12:12.097
and then be able to support them so that,

00:12:12.097 --> 00:12:15.537
academically we're not leaving
an entire generation behind,

00:12:15.537 --> 00:12:18.017
which is what so many
people are talking about.

00:12:18.017 --> 00:12:19.577
And that's still a choice to be made.

00:12:19.577 --> 00:12:22.857
Then of course, we have an entire focus

00:12:22.857 --> 00:12:27.297
on racial equity and
ensuring that our teachers

00:12:27.297 --> 00:12:29.127
are culturally competent teachers

00:12:29.127 --> 00:12:31.827
and able to support our kids

00:12:31.827 --> 00:12:33.657
in a way that's most responsible,

00:12:33.657 --> 00:12:36.670
that leads to the most learning
and engagement overall.

00:12:37.807 --> 00:12:39.877
- No, that makes a ton of sense.

00:12:39.877 --> 00:12:41.891
We're having questions
come in on social media

00:12:41.891 --> 00:12:45.324
from Facebook Livi Valanzuela asks,

00:12:45.324 --> 00:12:47.094
"Do you believe children will be behind

00:12:47.094 --> 00:12:49.764
"once we returned to
actual physical school?

00:12:49.764 --> 00:12:51.784
"What can I do as a parent?"

00:12:55.397 --> 00:12:58.727
- You know what, I know
you're familiar Sal

00:12:58.727 --> 00:13:00.967
with all the studies coming out

00:13:00.967 --> 00:13:02.737
from NWA McKinsey.

00:13:02.737 --> 00:13:05.329
So many folks who are
analyzing the situation

00:13:05.329 --> 00:13:08.217
and the truth of the matter is that

00:13:08.217 --> 00:13:11.297
without question kids on average

00:13:11.297 --> 00:13:15.527
are going to be behind and
especially children growing up

00:13:15.527 --> 00:13:16.687
in low-income communities

00:13:16.687 --> 00:13:18.087
who are disproportionally kids of color,

00:13:18.087 --> 00:13:21.107
who are already likely behind on average

00:13:21.107 --> 00:13:23.247
coming into schools.

00:13:23.247 --> 00:13:25.367
The fact that, as I said earlier,

00:13:25.367 --> 00:13:29.227
17 million kids still don't
have access to devices

00:13:29.227 --> 00:13:31.567
and connectivity.

00:13:31.567 --> 00:13:32.937
And that's only a part of it.

00:13:32.937 --> 00:13:35.147
As I know you have Sal

00:13:35.147 --> 00:13:38.787
that like technology for
technology sake is not the point.

00:13:38.787 --> 00:13:41.367
Once you even then have the technology,

00:13:41.367 --> 00:13:43.707
we then have to ensure that we are,

00:13:43.707 --> 00:13:47.277
developing our teachers on
how to then teach with this

00:13:47.277 --> 00:13:49.577
and ensure that that is in place.

00:13:49.577 --> 00:13:52.797
So our kids are able to engage.

00:13:52.797 --> 00:13:55.667
And so I do believe that,

00:13:55.667 --> 00:13:58.345
every indicator is
telling us that our kids

00:13:58.345 --> 00:14:00.917
are gonna be grossly behind.

00:14:00.917 --> 00:14:04.637
And so the things I think
we've gotta be focused on

00:14:04.637 --> 00:14:05.707
as number one,

00:14:05.707 --> 00:14:08.737
ensuring that everyone
truly does have access

00:14:08.737 --> 00:14:10.227
to a device and connectivity,

00:14:10.227 --> 00:14:12.137
because this isn't about this pandemic.

00:14:12.137 --> 00:14:13.937
This is about the future.

00:14:13.937 --> 00:14:16.777
This is just a tool that every kid needs.

00:14:16.777 --> 00:14:19.257
It is the basics like paper and pencils.

00:14:19.257 --> 00:14:20.697
We start about, everyone needs a backpack,

00:14:20.697 --> 00:14:22.637
a paper to start school.

00:14:22.637 --> 00:14:25.427
Everyone needs access to
a device and connectivity

00:14:25.427 --> 00:14:28.697
moving forward for us to
continue to find the path,

00:14:28.697 --> 00:14:32.087
to best educate all kids.

00:14:32.087 --> 00:14:34.717
And we've gotta train
our teachers on that.

00:14:34.717 --> 00:14:38.087
Also, we've got to ensure that we have

00:14:38.087 --> 00:14:41.070
information about what our kids need.

00:14:42.337 --> 00:14:45.397
There's a lot of questions
right now about testing,

00:14:45.397 --> 00:14:47.617
and should we be testing our kids or not?

00:14:47.617 --> 00:14:50.677
And it's often tied to accountability.

00:14:50.677 --> 00:14:53.017
And this is a moment to,

00:14:53.017 --> 00:14:55.344
reimagine how we think about testing

00:14:55.344 --> 00:14:57.517
and accountability for this year.

00:14:57.517 --> 00:15:01.507
What I feel strongly about
as imperfect as tests are,

00:15:01.507 --> 00:15:04.837
we really need to know where are our kids

00:15:04.837 --> 00:15:07.287
so that we can appropriately intervene

00:15:07.287 --> 00:15:08.811
so that we can be smart

00:15:08.811 --> 00:15:12.677
about our investments
in ensuring that kids

00:15:12.677 --> 00:15:14.177
get what they need.

00:15:14.177 --> 00:15:15.897
And then the last thing I'll say is

00:15:15.897 --> 00:15:17.887
we need to make sure
that we are advocating

00:15:17.887 --> 00:15:22.887
for dollars for funding during
the last great recession.

00:15:22.977 --> 00:15:26.607
We invested a lot more than
we have in this moment,

00:15:26.607 --> 00:15:27.717
through this crisis, right now,

00:15:27.717 --> 00:15:30.087
we have seen five times less funding

00:15:30.087 --> 00:15:31.707
coming into schools

00:15:31.707 --> 00:15:35.707
with a much greater challenge
with many complexities.

00:15:35.707 --> 00:15:37.117
So not only do we need to

00:15:37.117 --> 00:15:38.857
meet the basic needs of kids, right?

00:15:38.857 --> 00:15:41.367
I want you educators in schools

00:15:41.367 --> 00:15:43.047
right out of, after the pandemic

00:15:43.047 --> 00:15:44.887
figure out how are we gonna feed our kids?

00:15:44.887 --> 00:15:48.057
We thought, we were
realizing schools feed kids,

00:15:48.057 --> 00:15:53.057
schools provide all these
social nets for kids

00:15:53.096 --> 00:15:55.497
that suddenly are gone, safety,

00:15:55.497 --> 00:15:57.667
access to a clinic, et cetera.

00:15:57.667 --> 00:16:00.357
And so right away, our educators

00:16:00.357 --> 00:16:03.167
have been focused on meeting those needs.

00:16:03.167 --> 00:16:05.487
And we're gonna have
to continue to do that.

00:16:05.487 --> 00:16:09.337
And the trauma informed
teaching, but also,

00:16:09.337 --> 00:16:11.787
the academics and be smart about that

00:16:11.787 --> 00:16:14.257
and ensure that it's personalized.

00:16:14.257 --> 00:16:17.297
And that we're really
ensuring that parents

00:16:17.297 --> 00:16:19.747
and their families and kids

00:16:19.747 --> 00:16:22.667
and teachers, educators are
be able to work together

00:16:22.667 --> 00:16:24.257
to figure out how to close those gaps.

00:16:24.257 --> 00:16:26.930
So, as a parent, I would
just be asking questions

00:16:26.930 --> 00:16:30.007
about how do we know where our kids are

00:16:30.007 --> 00:16:31.517
and where investments need to go.

00:16:31.517 --> 00:16:33.227
And do have the investments needed

00:16:33.227 --> 00:16:37.127
to ensure that we're
able to educate all kids

00:16:37.127 --> 00:16:38.987
and invest in our
greatest natural resource

00:16:38.987 --> 00:16:41.420
we have in this country,
which is our students.

00:16:42.267 --> 00:16:46.307
- Absolutely and just to add
Livi to Elisa's response,

00:16:46.307 --> 00:16:48.327
which I 100%agree with,

00:16:48.327 --> 00:16:50.367
above and beyond everything
that the schools are doing.

00:16:50.367 --> 00:16:51.967
You ask what you can do as a parent,

00:16:51.967 --> 00:16:55.867
my advice there is, I don't
know how old your children are,

00:16:55.867 --> 00:16:57.027
but to Elisa's point,

00:16:57.027 --> 00:16:59.917
there's ways that you
evaluate where your kids are.

00:16:59.917 --> 00:17:01.127
You can go to Khan Academy,

00:17:01.127 --> 00:17:04.537
especially in math and
science and other areas,

00:17:04.537 --> 00:17:06.327
but you can use what we call

00:17:06.327 --> 00:17:08.267
our, get ready for grade level courses.

00:17:08.267 --> 00:17:10.417
They have something called
a course challenge in it.

00:17:10.417 --> 00:17:12.127
And let's say you have a child who's,

00:17:12.127 --> 00:17:14.607
I'm making it up, entering sixth grade,

00:17:14.607 --> 00:17:17.037
or just entered sixth
grade a few months ago.

00:17:17.037 --> 00:17:19.147
You can do the get ready for sixth grade

00:17:19.147 --> 00:17:20.467
course challenge.

00:17:20.467 --> 00:17:22.827
If they score 80, 90% on it,

00:17:22.827 --> 00:17:24.907
that means they don't
have any significant gaps

00:17:24.907 --> 00:17:26.267
from pre sixth grade.

00:17:26.267 --> 00:17:28.157
And then I would recommend them working

00:17:28.157 --> 00:17:31.667
at their own time and pace
20 to 40 minutes a day

00:17:31.667 --> 00:17:34.327
in math and personalized practice

00:17:34.327 --> 00:17:36.087
going through and trying to get mastery

00:17:36.087 --> 00:17:38.777
in all of sixth grade on Khan Academy.

00:17:38.777 --> 00:17:39.877
And if they are having trouble

00:17:39.877 --> 00:17:41.957
in the get ready for sixth grade,

00:17:41.957 --> 00:17:43.777
they can go and look at the units,

00:17:43.777 --> 00:17:45.607
or you can work with
them to look at the units

00:17:45.607 --> 00:17:46.677
where they have trouble

00:17:46.677 --> 00:17:48.447
and then they should get
mastering those gaps.

00:17:48.447 --> 00:17:50.967
And then they can go onto
the sixth grade work.

00:17:50.967 --> 00:17:51.800
I feel confident if they do that

00:17:51.800 --> 00:17:54.277
20, 30, 40 minutes a day

00:17:54.277 --> 00:17:56.547
over the course of the
school year and beyond

00:17:56.547 --> 00:17:57.557
they're gonna do just fine.

00:17:57.557 --> 00:17:58.997
They might even accelerate the learning,

00:17:58.997 --> 00:18:00.706
on the English Language Arts side

00:18:00.706 --> 00:18:02.307
there's tools, Khan Academy.

00:18:02.307 --> 00:18:05.067
You can use, there's four pay tools

00:18:05.067 --> 00:18:06.297
that are pretty good, too.

00:18:06.297 --> 00:18:08.387
Like Lexia, Raz-Kids.

00:18:08.387 --> 00:18:12.167
Once again, you can do
20, 30 minutes a day,

00:18:12.167 --> 00:18:13.652
but it can often be as simple as

00:18:13.652 --> 00:18:15.557
asking them to read a magazine article,

00:18:15.557 --> 00:18:16.407
depending on their age,

00:18:16.407 --> 00:18:18.887
read it and then we'll
discuss it at lunch.

00:18:18.887 --> 00:18:20.367
If you create those kinds of habits

00:18:20.367 --> 00:18:21.627
over the course of the year,

00:18:21.627 --> 00:18:22.827
and then the writing,

00:18:22.827 --> 00:18:24.857
just make sure they
don't atrophy that skill

00:18:24.857 --> 00:18:25.797
say, hey, once a week,

00:18:25.797 --> 00:18:27.147
I want you to write

00:18:27.147 --> 00:18:29.527
something that you want to change at home.

00:18:29.527 --> 00:18:30.487
Change your bedtime,

00:18:30.487 --> 00:18:31.507
something you wanna change in the world.

00:18:31.507 --> 00:18:32.607
We'll discuss it.

00:18:32.607 --> 00:18:34.767
That's make sure they don't atrophy.

00:18:34.767 --> 00:18:36.887
But those are just some
things you can make sure

00:18:36.887 --> 00:18:38.837
that if your kids do fine
just in your household,

00:18:38.837 --> 00:18:41.067
I have no doubt that they're
not going to fall behind.

00:18:41.067 --> 00:18:42.187
It's still gonna be suboptimal,

00:18:42.187 --> 00:18:43.537
but they won't fall behind.

00:18:44.707 --> 00:18:46.964
- Yes, yes, great advice.

00:18:46.964 --> 00:18:51.424
- There's a question here
from Facebook, Laura Jones,

00:18:51.424 --> 00:18:52.324
"Even students who are

00:18:52.324 --> 00:18:54.504
"straight A honors students are failing.

00:18:54.504 --> 00:18:56.064
"How do we help them recover from this?

00:18:56.064 --> 00:18:58.247
"Even with devices, kids are struggling."

00:18:58.247 --> 00:18:59.960
And Elisa I'll add to that question.

00:18:59.960 --> 00:19:01.417
What are y'all telling your.

00:19:01.417 --> 00:19:02.575
What are your teachers seeing?

00:19:02.575 --> 00:19:05.237
And what are you telling them for this?

00:19:05.237 --> 00:19:06.857
There's a teacher who
lives in my cul-de-sac,

00:19:06.857 --> 00:19:08.747
who has, I live in a middle-class,

00:19:08.747 --> 00:19:09.897
upper middle-class neighborhood

00:19:09.897 --> 00:19:13.047
where she also is seeing
five, 10% of her kids

00:19:13.047 --> 00:19:15.024
are completely disengaged right now.

00:19:15.024 --> 00:19:17.107
How are y'all coaching the teaching core

00:19:17.107 --> 00:19:19.667
to re-engage or keep these kids engaged

00:19:19.667 --> 00:19:21.257
and prevent the situation that Laura

00:19:21.257 --> 00:19:23.367
is talking about of a
former straight A student

00:19:23.367 --> 00:19:24.570
now failing classes?

00:19:25.687 --> 00:19:29.607
- Yeah, this is such an
important question Sal.

00:19:29.607 --> 00:19:32.827
And there's a few things
that come to mind.

00:19:32.827 --> 00:19:35.737
One is that we haven't hit on yet

00:19:35.737 --> 00:19:40.567
is I observed that a lot of our,

00:19:40.567 --> 00:19:42.737
the way we're trying to
approach virtual teaching

00:19:42.737 --> 00:19:45.521
is the way we approached, being in school,

00:19:45.521 --> 00:19:47.997
which really is not working for kids.

00:19:47.997 --> 00:19:51.347
There's just enough data and
information that we have.

00:19:51.347 --> 00:19:53.047
And if we should just go talk to our kids,

00:19:53.047 --> 00:19:55.877
parents talk to their kids
every day and you often hear

00:19:55.877 --> 00:19:57.317
it's boring.

00:19:57.317 --> 00:19:59.607
I don't understand the point of it.

00:19:59.607 --> 00:20:03.036
It just feels so irrelevant
and not pressing.

00:20:03.036 --> 00:20:07.207
And so this is an opportunity and at TFA.

00:20:07.207 --> 00:20:09.927
We talk about the need for
radical re-imagination.

00:20:09.927 --> 00:20:11.437
This is a time for creativity.

00:20:11.437 --> 00:20:13.497
This is time to say that isn't working,

00:20:13.497 --> 00:20:14.767
what is relevant to our kids?

00:20:14.767 --> 00:20:16.857
It's the work kids should be doing

00:20:16.857 --> 00:20:18.437
to be in context

00:20:18.437 --> 00:20:19.807
of what's happening in
the world right now,

00:20:19.807 --> 00:20:21.797
there's so much happening
in the world right now,

00:20:21.797 --> 00:20:24.527
that's helping to build their own,

00:20:24.527 --> 00:20:25.607
their knowledge and skills.

00:20:25.607 --> 00:20:28.400
I read something on the 74 million

00:20:28.400 --> 00:20:29.897
and they're doing these letters,

00:20:29.897 --> 00:20:31.917
student letters to adults

00:20:31.917 --> 00:20:35.377
saying, hello, here's
what we need and want.

00:20:35.377 --> 00:20:38.567
And examples, like get kids to,

00:20:38.567 --> 00:20:40.377
in your economics class to

00:20:40.377 --> 00:20:41.417
actually go figure out

00:20:41.417 --> 00:20:43.047
how are you gonna save the business

00:20:43.047 --> 00:20:46.767
that doesn't have the,

00:20:46.767 --> 00:20:49.287
because of COVID and the
shutdown of the economy

00:20:49.287 --> 00:20:50.397
is deeply struggling.

00:20:50.397 --> 00:20:53.027
What are the solutions
and what would you do?

00:20:53.027 --> 00:20:55.627
And what would you tell
the mayor or the governor?

00:20:55.627 --> 00:20:57.994
And just like real-world context

00:20:57.994 --> 00:21:00.887
that we can actually really grab onto

00:21:00.887 --> 00:21:02.633
and figure out how do we start

00:21:02.633 --> 00:21:04.927
ensuring that there's relevance,

00:21:04.927 --> 00:21:06.965
that one size fits all,

00:21:06.965 --> 00:21:09.257
doesn't work anymore for,

00:21:09.257 --> 00:21:11.327
to meet our kids where they are.

00:21:11.327 --> 00:21:13.547
And I don't think that
they're gonna show up

00:21:13.547 --> 00:21:16.387
unless they can see that this matters

00:21:16.387 --> 00:21:17.507
and is interesting

00:21:17.507 --> 00:21:22.147
and that they're actually
getting something for it.

00:21:22.147 --> 00:21:24.497
The other thing that we observed going on,

00:21:24.497 --> 00:21:26.607
as we talk to our own teachers

00:21:26.607 --> 00:21:28.670
is our kids have a lot going on.

00:21:30.067 --> 00:21:32.627
Our kids now are supporting
their own families.

00:21:32.627 --> 00:21:34.946
They've watched, their families lose jobs.

00:21:34.946 --> 00:21:38.037
And so, I observed some systems saying,

00:21:38.037 --> 00:21:39.527
you gotta show up for these hours

00:21:39.527 --> 00:21:40.794
and here's the expectation,

00:21:40.794 --> 00:21:44.097
but it's not in the context
of a global pandemic

00:21:44.097 --> 00:21:48.277
and the impacts of what
that is having on our kids,

00:21:48.277 --> 00:21:50.057
their families and their lives.

00:21:50.057 --> 00:21:54.477
And so we've just gotta
be more in conversation

00:21:54.477 --> 00:21:55.767
and in relationship

00:21:55.767 --> 00:21:58.227
with again families and
the students themselves

00:21:58.227 --> 00:22:01.067
to really understand their circumstances,

00:22:01.067 --> 00:22:03.208
to then be able to figure out,

00:22:03.208 --> 00:22:04.597
how do we adapt?

00:22:04.597 --> 00:22:06.667
I'm watching some of our school leaders

00:22:06.667 --> 00:22:08.057
get really present with that and

00:22:08.057 --> 00:22:11.147
say we can't expect kids
to be in school all day.

00:22:11.147 --> 00:22:11.980
They're not gonna do it.

00:22:11.980 --> 00:22:13.607
They actually have to go
work to support their family.

00:22:13.607 --> 00:22:17.387
And so instead, we're gonna
have these hours in the evening

00:22:17.387 --> 00:22:19.597
that seemed to work better six to nine,

00:22:19.597 --> 00:22:22.707
or when this is when we're
gonna actually host classes.

00:22:22.707 --> 00:22:25.947
And so just the need to really figure out

00:22:25.947 --> 00:22:29.187
how do we meet people where
they are in this moment.

00:22:29.187 --> 00:22:30.347
And that's what I'm observing

00:22:30.347 --> 00:22:33.057
of our network as well.

00:22:33.057 --> 00:22:34.867
There's this one school in St. Louis

00:22:34.867 --> 00:22:38.240
led by TFA alums, Kairos Academy.

00:22:38.240 --> 00:22:41.563
And they had the technology already.

00:22:41.563 --> 00:22:46.563
Their whole method was,
it's your round school.

00:22:46.743 --> 00:22:49.897
They do leased learning
chunks in seven weeks.

00:22:49.897 --> 00:22:51.177
And so they have the technology

00:22:51.177 --> 00:22:53.687
'cause they're big on
personalized learning,

00:22:53.687 --> 00:22:55.807
but they know that's just one tool.

00:22:55.807 --> 00:22:58.307
The thing they also have
is the relationships

00:22:58.307 --> 00:23:00.947
and the deep understanding
of how important that is

00:23:00.947 --> 00:23:02.697
to be able to execute.

00:23:02.697 --> 00:23:05.947
And so they were able to,
through this pandemic,

00:23:05.947 --> 00:23:07.597
they have pods,

00:23:07.597 --> 00:23:10.757
there's one adult to 10 kids
who are in relationship,

00:23:10.757 --> 00:23:12.307
understanding the needs.

00:23:12.307 --> 00:23:15.057
And that person also is
liaison to the families.

00:23:15.057 --> 00:23:16.957
And so there's a constant,

00:23:16.957 --> 00:23:19.767
adapting and ensuring that there's like,

00:23:19.767 --> 00:23:22.337
real understanding in order to ensure that

00:23:22.337 --> 00:23:24.217
we're able to keep kids engaged

00:23:24.217 --> 00:23:26.507
and their engagement
rate is through the roof.

00:23:26.507 --> 00:23:28.477
And so it takes us really

00:23:28.477 --> 00:23:31.027
thinking about the work
differently and understanding,

00:23:31.027 --> 00:23:34.667
and being empathetic to
what are our kids' needs

00:23:34.667 --> 00:23:36.230
and really present to that.

00:23:37.604 --> 00:23:40.487
- Absolutely and the only
thing I would add to that

00:23:40.487 --> 00:23:41.877
is depending on the age of the student,

00:23:41.877 --> 00:23:44.667
if you're talking about
someone pre high school,

00:23:44.667 --> 00:23:46.877
grades matter in that they're a signal

00:23:46.877 --> 00:23:48.947
and that you don't
wanna fail a whole grade

00:23:48.947 --> 00:23:49.780
and have to repeat it.

00:23:49.780 --> 00:23:50.857
But the important thing is,

00:23:50.857 --> 00:23:54.257
is that the child keeps
mastering the concepts.

00:23:54.257 --> 00:23:56.087
And as we mentioned,
there's tools to do that,

00:23:56.087 --> 00:23:57.547
to make sure they keep mastering.

00:23:57.547 --> 00:23:59.407
In high school, when the
stakes are a little bit higher

00:23:59.407 --> 00:24:01.931
in terms of your transcript
and college admissions,

00:24:01.931 --> 00:24:03.369
I would have a conversation.

00:24:03.369 --> 00:24:05.977
Definitely talk to the
child, talk to the student,

00:24:05.977 --> 00:24:07.510
but also talk to the school about it.

00:24:07.510 --> 00:24:09.407
Because I think right now you're seeing

00:24:09.407 --> 00:24:10.467
people are being,

00:24:10.467 --> 00:24:13.537
they understand that this
is a very weird situation

00:24:13.537 --> 00:24:15.617
and there should be more outlets for kids

00:24:15.617 --> 00:24:16.597
who maybe are failing

00:24:16.597 --> 00:24:19.037
or having struggling to eventually be able

00:24:19.037 --> 00:24:20.147
to get back on the horse

00:24:20.147 --> 00:24:21.517
and show that they know the material.

00:24:21.517 --> 00:24:25.897
But yeah, it's a real issue.

00:24:25.897 --> 00:24:27.787
Let's see, one more.

00:24:27.787 --> 00:24:29.097
We have time for one more question.

00:24:29.097 --> 00:24:31.827
This time goes by fast
and we're having fun so.

00:24:31.827 --> 00:24:33.954
From YouTube, SmartBear,

00:24:33.954 --> 00:24:36.317
"How do we help students re-enter school?

00:24:36.317 --> 00:24:38.314
"Before they were happy to enter schools

00:24:38.314 --> 00:24:39.964
"because they had friends
and there was no virus,

00:24:39.964 --> 00:24:42.024
"but now students will be
filled with fear to enter school

00:24:42.024 --> 00:24:43.227
"because of the pandemic."

00:24:43.227 --> 00:24:44.649
Well, I guess it could be,

00:24:44.649 --> 00:24:46.087
I think most people aren't thinking

00:24:46.087 --> 00:24:47.727
about seriously re-entering schools

00:24:47.727 --> 00:24:50.667
until at earliest the spring,

00:24:50.667 --> 00:24:51.570
maybe next school year.

00:24:51.570 --> 00:24:54.477
But I think even then
the question is relevant.

00:24:54.477 --> 00:24:56.187
People might not be
afraid of the virus then,

00:24:56.187 --> 00:24:58.257
but friendships might've been broken,

00:24:58.257 --> 00:24:59.577
all sorts of learning gaps.

00:24:59.577 --> 00:25:01.377
They're not even used to
the pattern of waking up

00:25:01.377 --> 00:25:03.337
and getting ready and
getting on the school bus.

00:25:03.337 --> 00:25:04.777
How do you think that's gonna play out?

00:25:04.777 --> 00:25:06.245
What should we do?

00:25:06.245 --> 00:25:08.657
- Well, so this is where I go back to,

00:25:08.657 --> 00:25:11.977
like my hope is that we don't just say

00:25:11.977 --> 00:25:13.397
everyone should go back to school

00:25:13.397 --> 00:25:15.087
and be doing school in
the same way, right?

00:25:15.087 --> 00:25:17.248
Because there are a segment of kids

00:25:17.248 --> 00:25:20.707
who are thriving in this environment

00:25:20.707 --> 00:25:22.777
who actually don't like

00:25:22.777 --> 00:25:24.224
the social and get intimidated

00:25:24.224 --> 00:25:27.987
and are able to just sort of clear the air

00:25:27.987 --> 00:25:30.367
and really thrive in this environment.

00:25:30.367 --> 00:25:33.328
And my question is why can't
that continue to be true

00:25:33.328 --> 00:25:37.577
and also figure out how
we can be doing schools

00:25:37.577 --> 00:25:39.487
and getting kids back with each other.

00:25:39.487 --> 00:25:43.117
We do have enough
research that shows that,

00:25:43.117 --> 00:25:45.797
the social development part
of this is really important,

00:25:45.797 --> 00:25:49.307
especially for the younger
kids and being back at school.

00:25:49.307 --> 00:25:51.117
My kids have been, my four sons

00:25:51.117 --> 00:25:52.347
and they've been back in school now

00:25:52.347 --> 00:25:54.507
for nearly 11 weeks.

00:25:54.507 --> 00:25:58.867
And they love going to school

00:25:58.867 --> 00:26:00.422
and they go to a Montessori school

00:26:00.422 --> 00:26:03.637
and they have really loved the opportunity

00:26:03.637 --> 00:26:06.397
to go back, it's smaller schools,
it's smaller class sizes.

00:26:06.397 --> 00:26:08.237
And they're able to,

00:26:08.237 --> 00:26:11.127
it's been interesting
to watch my kids thrive

00:26:11.127 --> 00:26:12.307
in the environment.

00:26:12.307 --> 00:26:14.717
That's a bit different
than what it was before,

00:26:14.717 --> 00:26:16.987
but I think we just have to
really be in communication

00:26:16.987 --> 00:26:19.537
with our kids and hopefully
thinking creatively

00:26:19.537 --> 00:26:23.517
of how do we do school now
that it got interrupted?

00:26:23.517 --> 00:26:25.537
What did work about that for,

00:26:25.537 --> 00:26:27.797
some kids, what didn't work about that

00:26:27.797 --> 00:26:31.417
and how do we really listen to students

00:26:31.417 --> 00:26:33.390
and families and communities?

00:26:34.447 --> 00:26:37.147
I think solutions are
within the community.

00:26:37.147 --> 00:26:39.017
And we often don't spend enough time

00:26:39.017 --> 00:26:42.177
really understanding that
to then inform our approach,

00:26:42.177 --> 00:26:45.127
which I just think is so key
to being able to do this well.

